<body><main><div class="eyebrow">SI 385 · University of Michigan</div><h1>NYC Restaurant Health Inspections</h1><p class="lead">Do independent restaurants really have worse inspection scores than chains? I revisited an existing analysis to see how much the answer changed when I made different decisions about the data.</p>
<div class="actions"><a href="../">Back to project guide</a><a href="../notebooks/DoU01_mariellj.py">View my source code</a><a href="../notebooks/DoU01_inherited.py">Original analysis</a></div>
<h2>Where I started</h2><p>The inherited notebook reported that independent restaurants scored about 44% worse than chains. In this dataset, a higher inspection score means more violation points. I wanted to see whether that gap would hold up when I changed how inspections were counted and how chains were defined.</p>
<div class="stats"><div class="stat"><strong>44%</strong><span>Original reported gap</span></div><div class="stat"><strong>~41%</strong><span>Gap after counting each inspection once</span></div><div class="stat"><strong>42 of 48</strong><span>Cuisines where independents had higher average scores</span></div></div>
<h2>First, I checked the rows</h2><p>I found that one inspection could appear multiple times because it had multiple violation records. The same inspection score was repeated on those rows. That meant some inspections could have more influence on the original average. After grouping the data so each inspection counted once, the reported gap dropped from about 44% to about 41%.</p>
<h2>Then, I changed the chain definition</h2><p>The data didn't have a ready-made chain label. The original analysis called a restaurant name a chain if it had at least 10 locations. I tried 5 and 20 locations too, keeping the rest of that comparison the same.</p>
<div class="panel"><div class="bar"><span>5+ locations</span><div class="track"><div class="fill" style="width:61.67%"></div></div><strong>37%</strong></div><div class="bar"><span>10+ locations</span><div class="track"><div class="fill" style="width:73.33%"></div></div><strong>44%</strong></div><div class="bar"><span>20+ locations</span><div class="track"><div class="fill" style="width:88.33%"></div></div><strong>53%</strong></div><p class="note">Reported difference in average inspection scores; bars use a shared 0–60% scale.</p></div>
<p>This was the biggest lesson for me: even without changing the inspection scores, changing the definition of a chain changed the headline.</p>
<h2>Finally, I looked within cuisines</h2><p>I compared chains and independents within cuisines where both were represented. Independents had higher average scores in 42 of the 48 comparable cuisines (87.5%). The overall pattern showed up in most cuisines, but not all.</p>
<h2>What I took away</h2><p>The pattern was fairly consistent, but the exact percentage depended on the choices made during analysis. These comparisons describe differences in scores; they don't prove that inspectors treated restaurants differently. I'd be careful about turning the original 44% into a policy claim without showing the sensitivity checks.</p>
